Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever an order or part of an order is returned (including a part return you have allowed as an exception), refund only what the customer actually paid for the returned item. That is its price minus its proportional share of any discount on the whole basket, so the share is by price and a ₹1,000 item in a ₹4,000 basket with ₹800 off carries ₹200 of the discount and refunds ₹800. Whenever a part return leaves less than a code's minimum spend, nothing is clawed back: the discount and any free delivery stay, and the customer gets only the discounted price of the returned item.
  2. Whenever an order is returned and it included a free gift item, refund only what was paid for the goods (for example ₹1,500) and do not ask for the free gift back; the customer keeps it.
  3. Whenever an order is refunded or cancelled, do not refund the delivery charge, unless the goods arrived damaged. This holds even when the cancellation is your own fault, such as a failed shop-error exchange.
  4. Whenever a refund is requested after the 30-day limit, refuse it and give nothing, whatever the reason (such as late courier delivery or the customer travelling). The cut-off is never bent.
  5. Whenever a refund to the customer's card bounces back (for example because the card has expired and been replaced), pay the amount as store credit instead.
  6. Whenever an order was paid partly by card and partly with points and it is returned in full, refund the card portion to the card and put the points portion back into the customer's points balance (for example ₹600 to the card and 400 points back).
  7. Whenever points are calculated for an order, base them only on what the customer paid for the items after any code, and leave delivery out. Whenever an order qualifies for a bonus-points promotion, give no bonus points, because no rule defines them.
  8. Whenever an order is delivered, credit its points on the delivery date, not at checkout (for example, bought on the 1st and delivered on the 5th means credited on the 5th).
  9. Whenever an order is refunded or returned, in full or in part, take back none of the points earned on it.
  10. Whenever an order that used a single-use code is fully refunded, do not give the code back, even if it has not expired. The used code is gone.
  11. Whenever a customer says a refund or points amount is wrong, nobody can change it. The system's calculation is final.
  12. Whenever a refund is requested for an item that is final-sale, refuse it and offer neither money back nor store credit, unless the item arrived damaged. Treat an order with no damage report on record as not damaged and refuse, even when the customer claims a defect (such as a broken zip).
  13. Whenever a customer who already bought an item asks for the difference because the item is now on sale at a lower price, and returns nothing, do not refund or credit the difference. There is no price-match or sale-difference policy, and the system's calculation is final.
  14. How many points does a customer earn on what they paid for items (after any code, delivery excluded)? -> B. One point for every full ₹50 paid for the items, which is 2 points per ₹100. So ₹1,800 gives 36 points, and ₹800 gives 16, not 8.
